{
    // Split-inlet scalar-mixing sensitivity case.
    //
    // Binary scalar (0 on the lower half of the inlet, 1 on the upper half,
    // expressed via the built-in `step()` expression function -- no
    // user.f90 needed) advects through a Brinkman design region and is read
    // out at the outlet by THREE scalar_mixing objectives:
    //   1. the lower-outlet quarter (y in [0, 0.25]), phi_ref = 0
    //   2. the upper-outlet quarter (y in [0.75, 1]), phi_ref = 1
    //   3. the whole outlet,                          phi_ref = 0.5
    // Advection-dominated transport keeps 1 and 2 near zero (the outer
    // quarters retain their inlet values) while 3 is large; a diffusive
    // regime drives everything toward 0.5.
    //
    // =====================================================================
    // EVERY NUMBER BELOW WAS RE-MEASURED AFTER THE INLET FIX.
    // =====================================================================
    // Until Neko c528651e9, `expression_dirichlet` never set `bc_type`, so
    // scalar_pnpn never marked the condition into bc_projector and the
    // imposed inlet value was overwritten by s = s + ds inside the same
    // timestep. This case therefore had NO scalar inflow whatsoever: what
    // reached the outlet was the decay of the initial condition, not a
    // mixing problem. Every Pe, end_time and cost figure derived before
    // that fix is void, and has been deleted here rather than annotated.
    // NEKO_DIR must carry that fix (see prepare.env); a measurement taken
    // against a Neko without it says nothing about this case.
    //
    // Pe = 1000: GATED ON THE MEASURED OUTFLOW DISTRIBUTION, not assumed.
    // Forward-only, run to steady state, 2 MPI ranks, at end_time 5.0 and
    // this resolution. obj1 is the lower-outlet quarter (phi_ref 0) and
    // obj3 the whole outlet (phi_ref 0.5):
    //
    //   Pe      obj1         obj3         regime
    //   2       1.200e-01    3.122e-05    diffusively saturated
    //   20      3.353e-02    1.805e-02    transitional
    //   200     4.027e-05    8.826e-02    advection-dominated
    //   1000    7.499e-13    1.088e-01    advection-dominated, strongly
    //   2000    4.640e-19    1.136e-01    below double precision
    //
    // Pe = 2 and Pe = 20 sit in the diffusive regime this case is not
    // testing: the outlet is nearly fully mixed, so the quarter objectives
    // are large and the whole-outlet one small -- the exact inversion of
    // the intended signature. Pe = 1000 reproduces the advection-dominated
    // signature strongly (the quarters hold their inlet extremes to
    // ~1e-12) and is the Pe of the known-good examples/unsteady_mixer
    // reference.
    //
    // Pe = 2000 was REJECTED DELIBERATELY, and NOT for instability. The
    // claim that used to sit here -- that 2000 is numerically unstable at
    // this dt -- is FALSE under the corrected configuration: re-measured,
    // it runs cleanly at dt = 1.0e-3 with a smooth, monotonic scalar
    // residual (0.657 -> 1.19e-6) and no NaN. It is rejected because its
    // quarter objectives reach 4.64e-19, three orders BELOW
    // double-precision epsilon (~2.2e-16) for a field valued in [0, 1].
    // Such a value is indistinguishable from round-off, and a sensitivity
    // that an MMA optimiser will differentiate must not be founded on one.
    // Pe = 1000's 7.5e-13 is small but still ~3000x epsilon, i.e. a real
    // number rather than accumulated noise. This is the non-obvious half
    // of the choice: the rejection is about representability, not
    // stability.
    //
    // phi_ref of 0 and 1 are maximally off-centre, so this case leans hard
    // on the backlog-#40 fix (b37ec1e on this branch) that made "phi_ref"
    // readable at all -- on any commit before that fix every one of these
    // three objectives would have silently run at the phi_ref=0.5 default,
    // making objectives 1 and 2 identical to 3. This case is the only
    // committed regression test for that fix.
    //
    // Mesh: non-periodic in y (unlike the other nine cases in this
    // directory, which share a periodic-y box) so the outlet actually has
    // a y in [0,1] extent to split into quarters -- generated as its own
    // 26x13-element box by run_sensitivity_regression_check.sh, since it
    // cannot share the shared periodic-y box (geometry differs) or that
    // box's coarser resolution (see below).
    //
    // RESOLUTION (moved here from the unit tier, which could not certify
    // this case at all -- TOLERANCE_UNREACHABLE, tol_min = 0.902, the FD
    // estimate not converging with eps across nearly a decade):
    //
    // polynomial_order was raised to 5 first, matching every sibling case
    // in this directory (dissipation.case, passive_scalar.case, ...) --
    // a spectral-element code gets far better dispersion behaviour per
    // degree of freedom from order than from `h` for advection-dominated
    // transport, so order was the first lever, not the last resort. At the
    // *shared* mesh resolution the other cases use (16x8 elements,
    // h = 0.125), P=5's minimum GLL spacing is 0.234944676/2 * 0.125 =
    // 0.01468 (ratio for the N=5 GLL nodes, see below). A *modest*
    // additional mesh bump (16x8 -> 26x13, i.e. ~1.6x linear, ~2.7x
    // elements -- not the large jump `h` alone would need without the
    // order increase) brings the minimum GLL spacing to
    // 0.234944676070535/2 * h (0.234944676070535 is the smallest of the 6
    // GLL node spacings on [-1,1] for a 6-point/order-5 element; halved
    // because it maps a length-2 reference interval), and at
    // h = 2/26 = 0.076923 that is 0.0090363. Kept at P=5 rather than
    // pushed further, and at this modest an N, because the explicit
    // viscous term that actually limits the time step scales as
    // nu*dt/dx_min^2 (see below), so a finer mesh or a higher order
    // tightens that limit quadratically while buying only accuracy per
    // DOF.
    //
    // CELL PECLET AT THE SELECTED Pe -- recorded as NOT met rather than
    // quietly dropped. The derivation above was made against Pe = 200 and
    // targets a cell Peclet (Pe * minimum nodal spacing) below ~2, which
    // it achieves there: 200 * 0.0090363 = 1.807. At the Pe = 1000
    // selected above, the same resolution gives 9.04, well over that
    // target. What was measured in its place, on this mesh at both
    // Pe = 1000 and Pe = 2000: the scalar residual falls smoothly and
    // monotonically to the steady simcomp's 1e-6 tolerance with no NaN and
    // no stagnation, and the objective values vary smoothly and
    // monotonically across the whole Pe ladder above -- none of the
    // wiggle-and-diverge signature of an under-resolved advection problem.
    // The mesh and polynomial_order were settled independently of Pe and
    // are deliberately left untouched here. If the FD certification below
    // ever degrades, under-resolution at this cell Peclet is the first
    // hypothesis to test, and it needs no edit to this file: raise
    // NEKO_TOP_SCALAR_MIXING_MESH_NX / _NY.
    //
    // dt and time_order: the derivation that used to sit here reasoned
    // from an advective CFL of ~0.11 and concluded that the fine mesh
    // forced dt = 2.5e-4. That was the wrong constraint. A root-cause
    // investigation -- reproduced in PURE NEKO, with no Neko-TOP in the
    // loop -- found the blowup is not advective at all, but comes from the
    // one explicitly-treated viscous term in pnpn:
    //
    //   fluid_pnpn.f90:701 builds the extrapolated velocity u_e by EXT
    //   extrapolation at order case.numerics.time_order, and
    //   pnpn_res_cpu.f90:80-96 takes its double curl and scales it by
    //   mu/rho into the pressure Poisson right-hand side. That
    //   nu*curl(curl(u_e)) term is the Karniadakis-Israeli-Orszag
    //   high-order pressure boundary condition, and it is the ONLY place
    //   nu appears explicitly -- everywhere else in pnpn the viscosity is
    //   implicit, inside the Helmholtz solve, and so unconditionally
    //   stable.
    //
    // The binding number is therefore viscous, and it lives at the
    // boundary: nu*dt/dx_min^2 = 12.2 here (Re = 1, so nu = 1, against the
    // minimum GLL spacing derived above), not the advective CFL of 0.11.
    // An explicit term sitting at 12.2 survives at all only because the
    // EXT extrapolation feeding it is damped at low order; the EXT gain is
    // 1, 3, 7 at time_order 1, 2, 3, so stability degrades sharply with
    // extrapolation order rather than with dt alone. That mechanism is a
    // property of the FLUID (Re), not of the scalar, so the Pe change
    // above leaves it untouched.
    //
    // Four measurements pin the mechanism, all at the resolution above:
    //   * time_order 3 diverges at step 271;
    //   * time_order 2 and time_order 1 both run clean;
    //   * time_order 3 at Re = 10 (nu 10x smaller) runs clean;
    //   * time_order 3 on a periodic-y box runs clean -- no y wall, hence
    //     no boundary for the KIO term to act on.
    // Lowering Re, or removing the wall, fixes it; lowering dt alone only
    // postpones it. Hence time_order = 2 here, and dt back at the ordinary
    // 1.0e-3 the rest of the suite uses.
    //
    // Margin at time_order 2 was measured, not assumed: stable at
    // dt = 1e-3, 2e-3 AND 4e-3, i.e. 4x headroom, where time_order 3 has
    // none whatsoever at 1e-3. Dropping the extrapolation order costs this
    // case nothing: it is a steady-state case driven to convergence by the
    // `steady` simcomp, so the temporal order affects only the path to the
    // converged state, not the converged answer the objectives read.
    //
    // Why this case alone: the five sibling cases registered in this suite
    // that carry an identical Re = 1 / time_order = 3 /
    // polynomial_order = 5 / timestep = 1e-3 combination
    // (viscous_dissipation, brinkman_dissipation, dissipation,
    // dissipation_weights and passive_scalar) are not stable by virtue of
    // any better-chosen number -- they survive because they all sit on the
    // shared periodic-y box, where y has no wall and the KIO term never
    // bites. scalar_mixing is the suite's only non-periodic-y case (it
    // needs a real y in [0,1] outlet to split into quarters), and is
    // therefore the only one exposed to this. Do not "fix" the siblings by
    // copying time_order 2 across; equally, any future non-periodic-y case
    // added to this directory inherits this constraint.
    //
    // Retained from the superseded derivation, because it is an
    // independent measured finding and still true: a JSON-only fix
    // (case.time.variable_timestep) was tried and rejected because
    // Neko-TOP's own simulation driver (sources/simulation/simulation.f90)
    // passes the *whole* case params object to time_step_controller%init
    // instead of the case.time subtree Neko's own neko.f90 extracts first,
    // so every variable-timestep key is looked up at the wrong JSON scope
    // and silently falls back to its Fortran default (huge()) -- confirmed
    // by the printed dt jumping to a nonsensical 1.0 rather than honouring
    // max_timestep. That is a Neko-TOP bug worth its own backlog entry,
    // not something to work around inside this case file.
    //
    // end_time = 5.0: MEASURED LADDER, not extrapolated. Forward-only,
    // 2 MPI ranks, this resolution, at Pe = 200:
    //
    //   end_time   obj1          obj3
    //   2.0        2.780e-05     8.957e-02
    //   5.0        4.027e-05     8.826e-02
    //   10.0       4.027e-05     8.826e-02
    //   15.0       4.027e-05     8.826e-02
    //
    // t = 5, 10 and 15 agree to ALL TEN printed significant figures, so
    // the objectives are read off a field that has genuinely stopped
    // moving; the `steady` simcomp reaches a scalar residual of 1.63e-6 at
    // t ~ 2.9. t = 2.0 is still ~30% out on obj1, so convergence really
    // does happen between 2 and 5 rather than immediately -- the ladder
    // brackets it rather than merely confirming a guess. 5.0 keeps ~1.7x
    // margin past convergence at negligible cost, and that margin is not
    // decoration: the FD sweep evaluates this case once per perturbation
    // with a PERTURBED design, and each of those trajectories has to
    // converge too, not just the baseline's.
    //
    // The old end_time of 185 came from the dead-inlet configuration,
    // where the only thing left to relax was the slow transverse-diffusion
    // decay of the initial condition (measured half-life 11.343, hence the
    // extrapolation to t ~ 183). With a live inlet the steady state is set
    // by advective washout through a domain of length 2 at u = 1, which is
    // why it is reached ~35x sooner. Nothing of the old figure survives.
    //
    // Convergence was re-confirmed AT THE SHIPPED Pe, not just at the
    // Pe = 200 the ladder was run at: in the FD sweep's own baseline
    // solve at Pe = 1000, steady_state_data.csv logs a scalar residual of
    // 1.768e-6 at t = 2.60 and then stops logging (the simcomp only logs
    // while unconverged, every 50 steps), so convergence lands in
    // t = (2.60, 2.65]. end_time 5.0 is ~1.9x that.
    //
    // COST: MEASURED at 2 MPI ranks (RECORD THE RANK COUNT alongside any
    // measurement here, since dFdx has been observed to differ by ~5.6e-7
    // between rank counts in this suite). One forward solve to t = 5.0 is
    // ~260 s; the adjoint solve is the expensive one at ~0.26 s/step
    // against the forward's ~0.05. The complete four-point FD sweep --
    // baseline, adjoint, 4 perturbed solves, 1 baseline restore -- was
    // timed end to end by CTest at 2336 s (39 min), against the 26-36 h
    // the pre-inlet-fix configuration implied. Note the `steady` simcomp
    // freezes only the FLUID on convergence (steady_simcomp.f90 carries
    // an explicit @todo for the scalar), so steps after t ~ 2.6 still
    // cost a scalar solve each -- cheap, but not free.
    //
    // A higher MPI rank count was tried and rejected for a measured
    // reason: this container's /dev/shm is capped at 64 MiB, and OpenMPI's
    // vader BTL requests a 16 MiB sm_segment per rank, so 8 ranks (128
    // MiB) cannot fit regardless of stale-segment cleanup. Measured
    // directly: an 8-rank run of this exact case failed after 123.6s with
    // repeated "not enough space" warnings, not a clean FD-tolerance
    // verdict -- see CMakeLists.txt.
    "version": 1.0,
    "case": {
        "mesh_file": "scalar_mixing_mesh/box.nmsh",
        "output_checkpoints": false,
        "output_at_end": false,
        "output_boundary": false,
        "time": {
            "end_time": 5.0,
            "timestep": 1.0e-3
        },
        "numerics": {
            "time_order": 2,
            "polynomial_order": 5,
            "dealias": true
        },
        "fluid": {
            "scheme": "pnpn",
            "Re": 1.0,
            "initial_condition": {
                "type": "uniform",
                "value": [ 1.0, 0.0, 0.0 ]
            },
            "velocity_solver": {
                "type": "cg",
                "preconditioner": {
                    "type": "jacobi"
                },
                "absolute_tolerance": 1e-16,
                "max_iterations": 50
            },
            "pressure_solver": {
                "type": "gmres",
                "preconditioner": {
                    "type": "hsmg"
                },
                "absolute_tolerance": 1e-12,
                "max_iterations": 50
            },
            "boundary_conditions": [
                {
                    "type": "velocity_value",
                    "value": [ 1.0, 0.0, 0.0 ],
                    "zone_indices": [ 1, 3, 4 ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "outflow",
                    "zone_indices": [ 2 ]
                }
            ],
            "output_control": "never"
        },
        "scalar": {
            "enabled": true,
            "Pe": 1000.0,
            "solver": {
                "type": "cg",
                "preconditioner": {
                    "type": "jacobi"
                },
                "absolute_tolerance": 1e-18,
                "max_iterations": 50
            },
            "initial_condition": {
                "type": "expression",
                "value": "step(y - 0.5)"
            },
            "boundary_conditions": [
                {
                    "type": "expression_dirichlet",
                    "value": "step(y - 0.5)",
                    "zone_indices": [ 1 ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "neumann",
                    "flux": 0.0,
                    "zone_indices": [ 2, 3, 4 ]
                }
            ]
        },
        "adjoint_scalar": {
            "enabled": true,
            "boundary_conditions": [
                {
                    "type": "dirichlet",
                    "value": 0.0,
                    "zone_indices": [ 1 ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "neumann",
                    "flux": 0.0,
                    "zone_indices": [ 2, 3, 4 ]
                }
            ],
            "initial_condition": {
                "type": "uniform",
                "value": 0.0
            }
        },
        "adjoint_fluid": {
            "initial_condition": {
                "type": "uniform",
                "value": [ 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ]
            },
            "boundary_conditions": [
                {
                    "type": "no_slip",
                    "zone_indices": [ 1, 3, 4 ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "outflow",
                    "zone_indices": [ 2 ]
                }
            ]
        },
        "simulation_components": [
            {
                "type": "steady",
                "tol": 1e-6,
                "compute_control": "tsteps",
                "compute_value": 1,
                "scalar_coupled": true
            }
        ],
        "point_zones": [
            {
                "name": "optimization_domain",
                "geometry": "box",
                "x_bounds": [ 0.2, 0.8 ],
                "y_bounds": [ 0.2, 0.8 ],
                "z_bounds": [ -1.0, 1.0 ]
            },
            {
                "name": "initial_ball",
                "geometry": "cylinder",
                "start": [ 0.5, 0.5, -0.5 ],
                "end": [ 0.5, 0.5, 1.5 ],
                "radius": 0.25
            },
            {
                "name": "outlet_low",
                "geometry": "box",
                "x_bounds": [ 1.8, 2.0 ],
                "y_bounds": [ 0.0, 0.25 ],
                "z_bounds": [ -1.0, 1.0 ]
            },
            {
                "name": "outlet_high",
                "geometry": "box",
                "x_bounds": [ 1.8, 2.0 ],
                "y_bounds": [ 0.75, 1.0 ],
                "z_bounds": [ -1.0, 1.0 ]
            },
            {
                "name": "outlet_full",
                "geometry": "box",
                "x_bounds": [ 1.8, 2.0 ],
                "y_bounds": [ 0.0, 1.0 ],
                "z_bounds": [ -1.0, 1.0 ]
            }
        ]
    },
    "optimization": {
        "fd_test_strict": true,
        // MEASURED, then set at ~2x the measured bias, per readme.md's
        // "Tolerances of the four unit cases" convention. The red half of
        // red-then-green is on record: this same sweep, at the provisional
        // 1.0e-6 that used to sit here, returned
        //
        //   p_hat 1.0143   C_hat +1.932e-3   branch FIT (estimate)
        //   3-term cross-check bias +1.926e-3   tol_min 5.018e-5
        //   BRACKETED, 4 truncation points, 1 sign crossing
        //   status TOLERANCE_UNREACHABLE, exit 1
        //
        // and the Richardson bias agreeing with the independent 3-term
        // cross-check to 0.3% is what makes C_hat trustworthy here.
        //
        // 4.0e-3 is 2.07x C_hat and 80x tol_min: reachable, and still
        // tight enough to enforce something. The measured bias is 0.19%
        // relative; a sign error, a missing chain-rule term or a dropped
        // adjoint forcing shows up as a relative error of order 1, i.e.
        // ~250x this tolerance. Do NOT read 4.0e-3 as "this gradient is
        // accurate to 0.4%" -- read it as "this gradient carries a
        // measured 0.19% bias that nobody has explained yet". The two
        // dissipation siblings sit at 4.5e-3 and 5.0e-3 over biases of
        // 2.2e-3 and 2.6e-3, the same order as this one, which is itself
        // evidence the residue is shared across the suite rather than
        // specific to scalar_mixing (adjacent to known-bugs #6).
        //
        // The single sign crossing inside the sweep is exactly why
        // fd_test_strict is on: the historical assertion reads the error
        // at the SMALLEST perturbation, which here is +1.563e-3, and would
        // have certified a number that is not the bias.
        "fd_test_tolerance": 4.0e-3,
        // Minimal viable sweep for the strict criterion at this case's
        // cost: 4 points is the floor for the order check ("needs at
        // least 4 sweep points"), spanning 0.3 down to 0.01 (geometric,
        // ratio ~3). MEASURED ADEQUATE, not assumed: it lands on the FIT
        // branch with a single order run covering all four points at
        // p_hat = 1.0143 -- inside the default 1.0 +- 0.10 band -- and
        // the harness reports the round-off upturn as BRACKETED, so the
        // sweep spans truncation and floor rather than sitting on one
        // side of the minimum. This case therefore behaves like the
        // FIT-branch siblings (viscous/brinkman_dissipation) and not like
        // the old unit-tier attempt, which never converged with eps.
        // Adding points would cost a full forward solve (~260 s) each and
        // buy this verdict nothing.
        "fd_test_perturbations": [
            3.0e-1, 1.0e-1, 3.0e-2, 1.0e-2
        ],
        "domain": {
            "type": "point_zone",
            "zone_name": "optimization_domain"
        },
        "design": {
            "type": "brinkman",
            "mapping": [
                {
                    "type": "PDE_filter",
                    "r": 0.05,
                    "tol": 1.0e-20,
                    "max_iter": 600
                },
                {
                    "type": "RAMP",
                    "f_max": 100.0
                }
            ],
            "initial_distribution": {
                "type": "point_zone",
                "base_value": 0.0,
                "zone_value": 1.0,
                "zone_name": "initial_ball"
            }
        },
        "solver": {
            "type": "mma",
            "max_iterations": 0,
            "tolerance": 1.0e-3,
            "mma": {
                "max_iter": 100,
                "asyinit": 0.5,
                "asyincr": 1.2,
                "asydecr": 0.7,
                "scale": 1.0,
                "auto_scale": false,
                "xmin": 0.0,
                "xmax": 1.0,
                "a0": 1.0,
                "a": 0.0,
                "c": 1000.0,
                "d": 0.0
            }
        },
        "objectives": [
            {
                "type": "scalar_mixing",
                "mask_name": "outlet_low",
                "phi_ref": 0.0,
                "weight": 1.0
            },
            {
                "type": "scalar_mixing",
                "mask_name": "outlet_high",
                "phi_ref": 1.0,
                "weight": 1.0
            },
            {
                "type": "scalar_mixing",
                "mask_name": "outlet_full",
                "phi_ref": 0.5,
                "weight": 1.0
            }
        ]
    }
}
